// llmsPage is the page `fitness llms` prints and llms.txt holds.
const llmsPage = "# fitness\n\nfitness runs a repo's fitness checks: small programs that each judge one rule, such as spelling, tests, or pull request shape. One binary holds every built-in check; a repo may add local checks. Each check decides whether it applies, so any repo runs the same suite with nothing to configure.\n\nA run resolves the checks, runs them side by side under time budgets, and prints a table of passed, failed, or info results. Info never fails a run. With files staged it judges only those files; --all judges every tracked file.\n\n## Exit codes\n\n- 0: every check passed, or a command succeeded.\n- 1: a check found problems, or the run could not start, such as a bad config.\n- 2: a command got arguments it does not take, or explain or docs named an unknown check or topic.\n\n## Learn the rest\n\n`fitness list` names every check, `fitness explain <check>` says why one exists and how to pass it, and `fitness docs` serves the guides. All of them, and this page, come from the code's own doc comments.\n\n## Run checks\n\n- `fitness` or `fitness <check> [args]`: Runs the full suite, or with a check name only that check, passing it the arguments after its name.\n- `fitness --all`: Forces a full scan even when files are staged.\n- `fitness --blob <text>`: Runs every blob-capable check on a text instead of the repo; - reads the text from stdin.\n- `fitness --dir=<folder>`: Judges only the files under one folder.\n- `fitness --json=<file>`: Names the file that receives the run's performance report: each check's time, budget, files, binary size, and error lines, as JSON.\n- `fitness --policy=external --checks=<name,name>`: Select the checks a command line names: with --policy=external, a run runs exactly the checks --checks lists.\n\n## Commands\n\n- `fitness llms`: Prints a page that teaches an AI the whole CLI.\n- `fitness list [--json]`: Names every check with its summary, scope, budget, and whether this repo enables it.\n- `fitness explain <check> [--json]`: Prints why a check exists, what it judges, how code comments count, and the config keys it reads.\n- `fitness docs [topic]`: Lists the guide topics, or prints one, from the code's doc comments.\n- `fitness check --list` or `fitness check <name> [args]`: Prints every built-in check name, one per line, or runs one check by name, a repo's own check included.\n- `fitness config [--json] [--policy=..] [--checks=..]` or `fitness config schema`: Shows the resolved settings and each check's state, or prints a JSON Schema for the config file.\n- `fitness init`: Installs the shared git hooks into this repo.\n- `fitness hook <name>`: Runs a git hook: commit-msg, pre-commit, or pre-push.\n- `fitness boxes <report.json> <page.html>`: Draws a --json report as an HTML box diagram.\n- `fitness strip-front-matter`: Deletes the fitness keys from tracked .md files' front matter, and any block they leave empty.\n- `fitness comments [path...]`: Prints each code comment block of the tracked files, or of those under the named paths, with its line, language and kind.\n- `fitness branch-check`: Judges this branch's PR and the issues it closes locally, posting nothing.\n- `fitness pr-check`: Judges pull request titles and descriptions, in GitHub Actions.\n- `fitness issue-check`: Judges an issue by its kind label and comments the result, in GitHub Actions.\n- `fitness plan-check`: Judges Plan issues only, as issue-check does, in GitHub Actions.\n- `fitness close-check`: Reopens an issue closed with unchecked items, or closes the parent it finishes, in GitHub Actions.\n- `fitness dupe-check`: Notes when a new issue resembles an older one, in GitHub Actions.\n- `fitness comment-check --blob <text>` or `fitness comment-check`: Judges a drafted comment, where - or no text reads stdin, or judges issue and PR comments and reply, in GitHub Actions.\n\n## Checks\n\n`fitness explain <check>` prints a check's whole documentation.\n\n- `adr-checks`: Keeps each architecture decision record in the README of the check that proves it.\n- `architecture-actors`: Requires each requirement's Given to start with an actor the system context declares.\n- `architecture-code`: Resolves each class of the code view to a declaration a language module's reader reads.\n- `architecture-components`: Gives every product source one owner among the components the components view draws.\n- `architecture-containers`: Matches the containers view against the runtime products the repository builds and deploys.\n- `architecture-contract`: Validates declarations beside all five architecture views before more specific checks resolve their proof.\n- `architecture-journey`: Keeps the user journey diagram and the requirement docs covering each other.\n- `build-output-untracked`: Keeps build output out of git and out of source imports.\n- `changelog`: Judges the `changelog/` entry files, the only changelog source.\n- `changelog-bullets`: Keeps changelog entries tight: every entry file's `###` section must have 3 to 5 bullets, each under 365 characters.\n- `changelog-updated`: Checks that each staged commit writes a changelog entry about its change.\n- `checkstyle`: Lints Java with the Checkstyle plugin the repo's own build declares, and holds its config to a complexity ceiling.\n- `comment-structure`: Judges one GitHub issue or PR comment, so a reply stays short, readable, and free of agent noise.\n- `commit-attribution`: Requires a commit message to disclose AI use in \"AI-Tools:\" and \"AI-Models:\" git trailers.\n- `cspell`: Fails unknown words in the checked files.\n- `dependency-currency`: Fails a declared npm dependency behind its latest published version — an installable update means the project isn't at peak fitness.\n- `doc-template`: Makes each markdown document match the shape of a template that governs it.\n- `duplicate-code`: Fails a repo whose duplicated lines pass 1% of its scanned lines.\n- `epic-structure`: Validates that an epic follows the Epic issue template, or the repo's own template labeled `Epic`.\n- `eslint`: Runs ESLint over the repo with the shared flat config, by executing the real ESLint binary.\n- `gitignore-why`: Requires every .gitignore pattern to follow a `#` comment explaining why it exists.\n- `go-complexity`: Enforces a cyclomatic complexity ceiling on Go functions, the Go-native counterpart of the house eslint rule (`complexity: max 5`).\n- `go-test`: Runs `go test ./...` in every Go module of the repo and reports the failing tests.\n- `go-test-coverage`: Measures entry coverage and test overlap.\n- `go-vet`: Runs `go vet ./...` in every Go module of the repo, catching suspicious code the compiler accepts.\n- `gofmt`: Lists Go files that gofmt would change.\n- `issue-checklist`: Fails an issue body whose task list still has unchecked items, so closing it would close unfinished work.\n- `issue-link-once`: Keeps each Issue's timeline readable (#153).\n- `java-test`: Runs Java tests once per build root and judges their JUnit XML results and JaCoCo coverage.\n- `java-version`: Checks a Java repo pins its JDK and that the machine's JDKs satisfy the pin, through the Java language module.\n- `local-branch`: Runs PR and Plan judgments locally.\n- `localized-text`: Fails SwiftUI text and requirement test literals that skip the app's String Catalog (#527).\n- `markdown-filename-camel-case`: Requires every markdown file name to be camelCase, standard root docs exempt.\n- `markdown-filename-kebab-case`: Requires every markdown file name to be kebab-case, standard root docs exempt.\n- `markdown-links`: Requires every relative link in every markdown file to resolve to an existing file or directory.\n- `markdown-no-bold-italic`: Fails bold and italic emphasis in markdown files: `**bold**`, `__bold__`, `*italic*`, or `_italic_`.\n- `mermaid-callout-why`: Requires each numbered callout table to carry a \"Why\" column, and flags numbered rows whose \"Why\" cell is empty.\n- `mermaid-callouts`: Requires every mermaid diagram with callouts to have a callout table whose callouts match it one to one.\n- `mermaid-diagram-prose`: Fails edge labels that carry prose beyond a callout, number or letter, in a diagram paired with a callout table.\n- `mermaid-diagram-table-gap`: Flags loose markdown prose wedged between a numbered mermaid diagram (or its legend block) and the callout table that follows it.\n- `mermaid-legend`: Requires numbered flowchart callout nodes to carry a style class, and diagrams with callouts to declare a `classDef` legend.\n- `mermaid-level-bleed`: Warns when a callout description at one C4 level repeats the previous level verbatim.\n- `mermaid-syntax`: Fails a mermaid diagram that would not render, so a broken diagram never reaches a reader.\n- `no-contrastive-reframing`: Flags the \"not X, it's Y\" reframing in markdown prose.\n- `no-eslint-disable`: Fails any JS or TS source file that holds an ESLint disable directive, in file, block, `-line`, or `-next-line` form.\n- `no-fitness-front-matter`: Fails a Markdown file whose front matter still holds `fitnessFunctions` or `relatedConfigurations`.\n- `no-plans-dir`: Keeps plans and temporary design work out of the repo.\n- `node-version`: Requires the machine's active Node version to satisfy the repo's `.nvmrc`.\n- `plan-structure`: Validates that a plan, a GitHub Issue body under the `Plan` label, follows the Plan issue template.\n- `plan-trailer`: Validates an optional `Plan #NN` commit trailer.\n- `pr-closes-issue`: Validates that a pull request description closes at least one issue on merge (#69).\n- `pr-structure`: Validates that a pull request description follows the PR template.\n- `prettier`: Runs Prettier `--check` over the repo's files, to ensure they are formatted.\n- `prose-budget`: Caps how much prose a markdown file holds.\n- `read-repo-first`: Reminds agents and users to read the repo's decisions and enabled checks first.\n- `release-changelog`: Binds Release Please's manifest to the version file a reviewer can inspect.\n- `repeated-string-literals`: Fails a string literal that appears three or more times across the repo's JS and TS sources.\n- `requirements`: Enforces docs/requirements in every repo a language module covers.\n- `requirements-changed`: Fails a change that adds or changes product code without also changing a test and a requirement doc.\n- `semantic-commit`: Requires the commit subject to follow Conventional Commits, `type(scope): description`, with the scope required.\n- `spotless`: Checks Java formatting with the Spotless plugin the repo's own build declares.\n- `swiftlint`: Runs SwiftLint over the repo through the Swift language module, which owns the tool run and its judgment, via `swiftlint lint --strict`.\n- `text-readability`: Fails a markdown file whose prose scores as hard to read.\n- `vitest-coverage-exclude`: Fails any Vitest coverage exclude entry, so coverage measures every file.\n- `vitest-coverage-full`: Requires Vitest coverage thresholds of 100 in the repo and the shared config, then runs `vitest run --coverage`.\n- `workflow-ref-exists`: Fails a workflow calling a may-journal workflow or action at a tag or sha lacking that path (#485).\n- `workflow-reporting`: Fails a workflow job that can fail without a report on its run page (#181).\n\n## Topics\n\n- `fitness docs config`: Package conf loads .fitnessrc.json — the Go runner's configuration file.\n- `fitness docs coverage`: Package goal holds the coverage goal every coverage check judges by, whichever language it measures: every counted line, branch or statement covered.\n- `fitness docs github`: Package metadata shares GitHub judgments between local checks and Actions.\n- `fitness docs hooks`: Package hooks embeds the canonical may-journal git hooks that `fitness init` installs into a consumer repo.\n- `fitness docs languages`: Package lang defines what a language module brings to a run and orders what modules claim against what is installed or embedded.\n- `fitness docs local-checks`: Package repocheck finds a repo's own checks (ADR 0009, ADR 0012) and builds the Go ones, as docs/local-checks.md describes.\n- `fitness docs protocol`: Package checkkit is the contract between the fitness runner and a check binary, which prints one JSON Result on stdout.\n"
